mmendation":"Administer clindamycin for GBS prophylaxis and morphine as ordered for pain."}],"vitals":[],"io":[],"assessments":[],"mar":[],"medicationCatalog":[{"id":"med-1790012189205-rvv1x","patientId":"amelia-sung","name":"Oxytocin (Pitocin)","dose":"30 units in 500 mL sodium chloride 0.9%; start at 2 milliunits/min","route":"IV infusion","frequency":"Increase by 2 milliunits/min every 15 minutes until contractions are every 2–3 minutes","scheduledTime":"Active labor","status":"Due","provider":"Dr. Darnell","highAlert":true,"releaseStatus":"released","barcode":"1013","notes":"","sourceOrderId":"amelia-order-oxytocin"},{"id":"med-1790012189206-ncbam","patientId":"amelia-sung","name":"Lactated Ringer’s solution (LR)","dose":"125 mL/hr","route":"IV infusion","frequency":"Continuous","scheduledTime":"Now","status":"Due","provider":"Dr. Darnell","highAlert":false,"releaseStatus":"released","barcode":"1014","notes":""},{"id":"med-1790012189206-uka4u","patientId":"amelia-sung","name":"Clindamycin (Cleocin)","dose":"900 mg","route":"IVPB","frequency":"Every 6 hours until delivery for GBS prophylaxis","scheduledTime":"Now","status":"Due","provider":"Dr. Darnell","highAlert":false,"releaseStatus":"released","barcode":"1015","notes":"","sourceOrderId":"amelia-order-clindamycin"},{"id":"med-1790012189206-wgbhh","patientId":"amelia-sung","name":"Morphine sulfate (Duramorph)","dose":"2 mg","route":"Slow IV push","frequency":"Every 2 hours PRN pain","scheduledTime":"PRN","status":"Due","provider":"Dr. Darnell","highAlert":true,"releaseStatus":"released","barcode":"1016","notes":"","sourceOrderId":"amelia-order-morphine"},{"id":"med-1790012189206-fkca4","patientId":"amelia-sung","name":"Lidocaine (Xylocaine) 1%","dose":"Available at delivery","route":"Local infiltration","frequency":"As needed at delivery","scheduledTime":"Delivery","status":"Due","provider":"Dr. Darnell","highAlert":false,"releaseStatus":"released","barcode":"1017","notes":"","sourceOrderId":"amelia-order-lidocaine"}],"bloodUnits":[],"glucoseChecks":[],"laborProgress":[],"postpartumRecovery":[],"pphPads":[],"pphMedications":[],"bloodAdministration":[],"surgicalChecklist":[],"surgicalAssessments":[],"diagnosticFiles":[],"labPanels":[],"chartEntries":[],"pewsAssessments":[],"messages":[],"notifications":[]},"releaseQueue":[],"marVisibility":true,"marHiddenRecords":{},"scenarioStage":null},"jane-fowler":{"patient":{"id":"jane-fowler","name":"Jane Fowler","mrn":"SIM-204051","dob":"01/28/XXXX","room":"204","provider":"Dr. Smith","allergies":["NKDA"],"weightKg":60,"bloodType":"A+","codeStatus":"Full Code","age":"79","sex":"Female","primaryDiagnosis":"Ovarian cancer — preoperative total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy","surgery":"Total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy and surgical staging","scenario":"Postoperative opioid intoxication / respiratory depression","barcode":"1001"},"chartRecords":[{"id":"chart-jane-fowler-overview","patientId":"jane-fowler","title":"Patient Overview","category":"summary","status":"released","content":"<columns>\n\t<column ratio=\"33.33\">\n\t\tDOB:  01/28/XXXX\n\n\t</column>\n\t<column ratio=\"33.33\">\n\t\tAGE: 79 years old\n\t</column>\n\t<column ratio=\"33.33\">\n\t\tWT: 60 KG\n\t</column>\n</columns>\nPrimary Diagnosis: Ovarian Cancer:\nSurgery: Hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-opherectomy\n<callout icon=\"✅\" color=\"blue_bg\">\n\tAllergies: NKDA\n</callout>"},{"id":"chart-1d0195d201d581799173e0d011057fde","patientId":"jane-fowler","title":"CT Results","category":"labs","status":"released","content":"![](assets/fe6431c4023fdd7641.png)\n\n**CT ABDOMEN AND PELVIS**\n**Patient:** Jane Fowler\n**Referring Physician:** Dr. Smith\n**Clinical Indication:** Abdominal pain and evaluation of suspected adnexal mass; concern for ovarian neoplasm.\n<span underline=\"true\">**Findings:**</span>\n**Liver, Gallbladder, Biliary Tree:**\nNormal in size and attenuation. No focal hepatic lesions or biliary ductal dilation.\n**Pancreas:**\nUnremarkable. No masses identified.\n**Spleen:**\nNormal in size and appearance.\n**Kidneys and Adrenals:**\nBoth kidneys are normal in size and contour; no masses, stones, or hydronephrosis. Adrenal glands are unremarkable.\n**Bowel and Mesentery:**\nNo evidence of bowel obstruction or wall thickening. Mesenteric fat planes are preserved.\n**Lymph Nodes:**\nNo pathologically enlarged lymph nodes in the abdomen or pelvis.\n<span color=\"yellow_bg\">**Uterus and Adnexa:**</span>\n<span color=\"yellow_bg\">A heterogenous soft-tissue mass is seen in the right adnexal region measuring approximately 3.12 cm. The mass demonstrates solid components with irregular margins and is in close relation to the right ovary, concerning for tumor involvement or direct invasion of the right ovary. The left ovary appears unremarkable. The uterus is normal in size and contour.</span>\n**Peritoneal Cavity:**\nNo definite peritoneal implants or omental caking identified."},{"id":"chart-1d0195d201d581cf8a0adf7495447769","patientId":"jane-fowler","title":"Postoperative Progress Note","category":"surgery","status":"pending","content":"###\n\n### **Postoperative Progress Note**\n**Patient Name:** Jane Fowler\n**DOB:** 01/28/XXXX\n**Age/Sex:** 79-year-old female\n**Date of Surgery:** Today\n**Surgeon:** Dr. Smith\n**Procedure:** **Total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy**\n\n### **Subjective**\nPatient seen postoperatively and is awake, alert, and resting comfortably. She reports mild abdominal discomfort, which is currently well controlled. Denies nausea, vomiting, chest pain, or shortness of breath.\n\n### **Objective**\nPatient is status post total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy performed under general anesthesia. The procedure was completed without intraoperative complications. Estimated blood loss was approximately 400 mL. Intraoperatively, two tumors were identified and removed, measuring 1.23 cm and 3.25 cm, respectively. The uterus, bilateral ovaries and fallopian tubes, tumors, and all associated surgical specimens were sent to pathology for evaluation.\nThe patient received 2 liters of Lactated Ringers intraoperatively, with IV Lactated Ringers currently infusing at 125 mL/hr. She was extubated in the operating room and is breathing spontaneously with stable vital signs. Abdominal incision is covered with a 4×4 gauze dressing that is clean, dry, and intact with no drainage noted. Indwelling urinary catheter remains in place with approximately 200 mL urine output documented postoperatively. Sequential compression devices are in place.\n\n### **Assessment**\n79-year-old female status post uncomplicated **total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy** with removal of two tumors. Hemodynamically stable with appropriate urine output and no immediate postoperative complications identified.\n\n### **Plan**\n1. **Postoperative Care**\n\t- Continue routine postoperative monitoring\n\t- Post-op vital signs per protocol\n\t- Titrate oxygen to maintain pulse oximetry of 98% or greater.\n2. **Fluids**\n\t- Continue **IV Lactated Ringers at 125 mL/hr**\n3. **Diet**\n\t- Sips of water; advance to clear liquids as tolerated\n4. **Pain Control**\n\t- **Morphine sulfate (Duramorph) 2 mg IV push PRN pain**, may titrate up to **10 mg every 4 hours** as needed\n5. **Nausea**\n\t- **Ondansetron (Zofran) 4 mg IV push every 4 hours PRN**\n6. **Activity**\n\t- Out of bed this evening as tolerated\n7. **GU**\n\t- Maintain Foley catheter\n\t- Strict intake and output every 4 hours; notify provider for urine output \\<30 mL/hr\n8. **DVT Prophylaxis**\n\t- Continue sequential compression devices\n9. **Consults**\n\t- Consult oncology in AM.\n10. **Pathology**\n- Await pathology results on surgical specimens\n\n- Transfer to surgical floor for continued postoperative care","releaseWith":"chart-2d6195d201d58030b0ded95695bbcb1e"},{"id":"chart-1d0195d201d581d49553e7787bbe1924","patientId":"jane-fowler","title":"Admission Orders","category":"orders","status":"released","content":"## Jane Fowler — Admission Orders\n\n**Provider: Dr. Smith MD**\n\n1. Admit to Surgical Pre-Op for preoperative total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy.\n2. Vital signs every 30 minutes until surgery.\n3. Diet: NPO.\n4. Code status: Full Code.\n5. Obtain sterilization consent, blood-administration consent, and surgical consent for total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy.\n6. Lactated Ringer’s solution (LR) IV at 125 mL/hr.\n7. Obtain CBC, urine hCG, BMP, and blood type.\n8. Insert indwelling Foley catheter.\n9. Apply bilateral sequential compression devices (SCDs).\n10. Cefazolin (Ancef) 2 g in 250 mL IV on call to the operating room; infuse over 1 hour.\n11. Metoclopramide (Reglan) 10 mg IV; available concentration 10 mg/2 mL.\n12. Midazolam (Versed) 1 mg IV on call to the operating room; available concentration 5 mg/mL.\n13. Prepare the abdomen: shave as needed and cleanse with antibacterial wipes.\n14. Complete the surgical checklist and time-out documentation.\n15. Teach postoperative incision splinting and incentive-spirometer use."},{"id":"chart-2d6195d201d58030b0ded95695bbcb1e","patientId":"jane-fowler","title":"Post-Op Progress Notes & Orders","category":"orders","status":"pending","content":"## Post-Op Orders\n\n**Provider: Dr. Smith MD**\n\n- Diet: Sips of water; advance to clear liquids as tolerated.\n- Activity: Out of bed tonight.\n- Postoperative vital signs per protocol.\n- Titrate oxygen to maintain pulse oximetry of 98% or greater.\n- Lactated Ringer’s solution (LR) IV at 125 mL/hr.\n- Continue sequential compression devices (SCDs).\n- Maintain indwelling Foley catheter.\n- Intake and output every 4 hours; notify the provider for urine output less than 30 mL/hr.\n- Morphine sulfate (Duramorph) 2 mg IV push PRN pain; may repeat up to 10 mg every 4 hours. Available concentration: 2 mg/1 mL.\n- Ondansetron (Zofran) 4 mg IV push every 4 hours PRN nausea. Available concentration: 4 mg/2 mL.\n- Consult oncology in AM.\n- Transfer to the surgical floor."},{"id":"chart-2d6195d201d580cf95cadb254eafc611","patientId":"jane-fowler","title":"Admission labs-Fowler","category":"labs","status":"released","content":"## **PATIENT INFORMATION**\n<table header-row=\"true\">\n<tr>\n<td>**Name:** Jane Fowler</td>\n<td>**Gender Identification:** Female</td>\n<td>**Allergies:** No known allergies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**DOB:** 1/28/XX</td>\n<td>**Age:** 79 years</td>\n<td>**Adm Provider:** Dr. Smith</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Diagnosis:** pre-op hysterectomy</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n\n## **LABORATORY REPORT**\n**Specimen Collected:** Admission\n\n### **Venous Blood Analysis**\n### **Complete Blood Count (CBC)**\n<table header-row=\"true\">\n<tr>\n<td>Test (Reference Range)</td>\n<td>**Admission**</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Hgb (female 12–16 g/dL)**</td>\n<td>11.8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**HCT (female 36–48%)**</td>\n<td>36</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**WBC (4.5–10.5 ×10⁹/L)**</td>\n<td>11.5\\*</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Platelets (150–400 ×10⁹/L)**</td>\n<td>220</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n\n### **Basic Metabolic Panel (BMP)**\n<table header-row=\"true\">\n<tr>\n<td>Test (Reference Range)</td>\n<td>**Admission**</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Na⁺ (136–145 mEq/L)**</td>\n<td>140</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**K⁺ (3.5–5.5 mEq/L)**</td>\n<td>4.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Cl⁻ (98–106 mEq/L)**</td>\n<td>104</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**HCO₃⁻ (23–30 mEq/L)**</td>\n<td>25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**BUN (8–20 mg/dL)**</td>\n<td>18</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Creatinine (female 0.4–1.0 mg/dL)**</td>\n<td>1.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Glucose (70–110 mg/dL)**</td>\n<td>108</td>\n</tr>\n<tr color=\"yellow_bg\">\n<td>Blood type </td>\n<td>A+</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n\n### **Miscellaneous**\n<table header-row=\"true\">\n<tr>\n<td>Test</td>\n<td>**Admission**</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Pregnancy Test (hCG)**</td>\n<td>Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>**Urinalysis**</td>\n<td>Negative</td>\n</tr>\n</table>"},{"id":"chart-2fd195d201d58013ab8bffdf475123e4","patientId":"jane-fowler","title":"History and Physical","category":"notes","status":"released","content":"## History and Physical\n\n**Date of Admission:** Today\n**Patient Name:** Jane Fowler\n**Chief Complaint:** Pelvic pressure, bloating, and constipation\n**DOB:** 01/28/XXXX\n**Admitting Physician:** Dr. Smith\n**Age/Sex:** 79-year-old female\n**Source of History:** Patient and daughter\n\n### History of Present Illness\n\nJane Fowler has been experiencing pelvic pressure, bloating, and constipation. Her primary provider could palpate her right ovary. An abdominal CT scan showed a tumor with possible invasion of the right ovary. She is admitted for a total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy and surgical staging today.\n\n### Past Medical History\n\n- No history of surgeries\n- No significant medical history\n\n### Allergies\n\n- NKDA\n\n### Social History\n\n- Lives alone\n- No tobacco, alcohol, or drug use\n\n### Home Medications\n\n- Acetaminophen (Tylenol) 650 mg as needed\n- Polyethylene glycol 3350 (MiraLAX) daily\n- Docusate sodium (Colace) daily\n- Melatonin (Natrol) 3 mg every night\n- Escitalopram (Lexapro) 10 mg PO daily\n\n### Assessment\n\nShe is alert and oriented to time, person, place, and situation. Heart rate and rhythm are regular. Lungs are clear to auscultation; oxygen saturation is 97% on room air. The abdomen is slightly distended and tender to light palpation, with rebound tenderness present.\n\n### Plan\n\n1. Total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy and surgical staging.\n   - Cefazolin (Ancef) 2 g IV once on call to the operating room.\n2. Hydration and preoperative care.\n   - Lactated Ringer’s solution (LR) IV at 125 mL/hr.\n   - NPO.\n   - Sequential compression devices.\n   - Indwelling Foley catheter.\n3. Disposition.\n   - Admit to the medical-surgical floor for close monitoring."},{"category":"mar","content":"<callout icon=\"🎫\" color=\"blue_bg\">\n\tYou must enter any newly ordered medications on the MAR to chart that they are given.\n</callout>\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col width=\"390\">\n<col>\n<col>\n<col>\n</colgroup>\n<tr>\n<td>Medication </td>\n<td>Time Due</td>\n<td>Last Time given/Initials</td>\n<td>Time given/Initials</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Morphine sulfate (Duramorph) 2mg IVP as needed for pain; may repeat/titrate to a maximum of 10 mg per dose, not to exceed more that 10mg every 4 hours. </td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>14:26/SF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zofran 4 mg IVP every 4 hours as needed for nausea and vomiting.</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>14:45/SF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IV: LR @125ml/hr </td>\n<td></td>\n<td>Running</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ketorolac (Toradol) 30mg IV push STAT</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>14:45/SF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Narcan 1mL IV</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>14:31/SF</td>\n</tr>\n\n</table>","id":"chart-2d6195d201d58077a080faf182983607","patientId":"jane-fowler","status":"pending","title":"Postoperative MAR"},{"id":"admin-jane-ct-results","patientId":"jane-fowler","title":"CT Abdomen and Pelvis","category":"labs","status":"released","content":"![Jane Fowler CT abdomen and pelvis](assets/jane-fowler-ct.png)\n\n**CT Abdomen and Pelvis Result**\n\nLarge right ovarian tumor with possible local invasion. Findings correlate with the patient’s pelvic pressure, bloating, constipation, and palpable right ovary."},{"id":"admin-jane-respiratory-naloxone","patientId":"jane-fowler","title":"Naloxone (Narcan) 0.2 mg IV push","category":"orders","status":"pending","content":"Provider: Dr. Smith MD\n\nNaloxone (Narcan) 0.2 mg IV push every 2–3 minutes PRN respiratory rate less than 6/min or change in level of consciousness. Available concentration: 0.4 mg/mL."},{"id":"admin-jane-respiratory-ketorolac","patientId":"jane-fowler","title":"Ketorolac (Toradol) 30 mg IV push","category":"orders","status":"pending","content":"Provider: Dr. Smith MD\n\nKetorolac (Toradol) 30 mg IV push once now. Available concentration: 30 mg/mL."},{"id":"admin-jane-faculty-guide","patientId":"jane-fowler","title":"Jane Fowler Faculty Simulation Guide","category":"faculty","status":"released","content":"Shift 1\n\nSituation\nJane Fowler has experienced pelvic pressure, bloating, and constipation. Her primary provider palpated her right ovary. CT showed a right ovarian tumor with possible invasion. She is scheduled for a total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy and surgical staging.\n\nStarting findings\nT 98.9 F; HR 89; RR 20; BP 124/76; SpO2 97%. Alert and oriented x4; moves all extremities on command; denies pain; normoactive bowel sounds; clear breath sounds.\n\nStudent expectations\nIntroduce self; perform hand hygiene; review orders; verify two identifiers; complete initial assessment and vital signs; explain the plan of care; begin IV fluids; verify consents and complete the pre-op checklist; insert the urinary catheter; teach incentive spirometry, leg exercises, splinting, coughing, and deep breathing; administer the ordered antibiotic using medication rights.\n\nShift 2\n\nSituation\nPostoperative total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy under general anesthesia. The patient tolerated surgery without complications. Abdominal incision is covered with a 4 x 4 gauze dressing with no drainage. Lactated Ringer's solution is infusing at 125 mL/hr after 2 L received during surgery. Estimated blood loss was 400 mL. She was extubated in the operating room and is breathing spontaneously. Foley catheter is present with 200 mL urine output.\n\nStarting findings\nT 97.9 F; HR 98; RR 17; BP 143/86; SpO2 93%. Pale; responds to name; moves extremities on command; moaning; hypoactive bowel sounds; clear breath sounds.\n\nStudent expectations\nIntroduce self; perform hand hygiene; review orders; verify two identifiers; complete the initial assessment and apply cardiopulmonary monitoring; recognize the low SpO2 and apply oxygen; assess pain; explain the plan of care. When the patient reports pain 6/10, administer the released analgesic using medication rights. If the patient becomes unresponsive with RR 6 and SpO2 85%, recognize respiratory depression, begin bag-mask ventilation, notify anesthesia, administer released rescue medications, reassess, and monitor stability."}],"collections":{"orders":[{"id":"jane-preop-admit","patientId":"jane-fowler","time":"Admission","provider":"Dr. Smith MD","type":"Admission","text":"Admit to Surgical Pre-Op for preoperative total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy.","status":"Active","enteredBy":"Provider"},{"id":"jane-preop-vitals","patientId":"jane-fowler","time":"Admission","provider":"Dr. Smith MD","type":"Nursing","text":"Vital signs every 30 minutes until surgery.","status":"Active","enteredBy":"Provider"},{"id":"jane-preop-lr","patientId":"jane-fowler","time":"Admission","provider":"Dr. Smith MD","type":"IV / Fluids","text":"Lactated Ringer's solution (LR) at 125 mL/hr.","status":"Active","enteredBy":"Provider"},{"id":"jane-preop-npo","patientId":"jane-fowler","time":"Admission","provider":"Dr. Smith MD","type":"Diet","text":"NPO.","status":"Active","enteredBy":"Provider"},{"id":"jane-preop-code","patientId":"jane-fowler","time":"Admission","provider":"Dr. Smith MD","type":"Code Status","text":"Full Code.","status":"Active","enteredBy":"Provider"},{"id":"jane-preop-consents","patientId":"jane-fowler","time":"Admission","provider":"Dr. Smith MD","type":"Consent","text":"Obtain sterilization consent, blood-administration consent, and surgical consent for total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy.","status":"Active","enteredBy":"Provider"},{"id":"jane-preop-labs","patientId":"jane-fowler","time":"Admission","provider":"Dr. Smith MD","type":"Laboratory","text":"Obtain CBC, urin